\begin_layout Title
Classical perturbation theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
vspace{2.5em}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 be the matrix under study and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}=\bm{A}+\bm{E}$
\end_inset

 be the perturbed matrix, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 represents the perturbation.
 The central question of the current monograph is: How does the eigenvalues
 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

singular values) and eigenvectors (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

singular vectors) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 change in response to the perturbation
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Mathematical backgrounds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before presenting the classical matrix perturbation theory, we first review
 some basic facts in matrix analysis and probability theory that are used
 extensively in the coming sections.
 The readers familiar with these materials can proceed directly to Section
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:classical-perturbation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix analysis 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Matrix-analysis"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Eigenvalues and eigenvectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We start with the definition of eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a square
 matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{definition}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Eigenpair
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "def:eigenpair"

\end_inset

 Fix a square matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

.
 We say 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda,\bm{v})\in\mathbb{C}\times\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is an 
\emph on
eigenpair
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{v}$
\end_inset

 is nonzero and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{A}\bm{v}=\lambda\bm{v}.\label{eq:def-eigenpair}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In particular, the scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is an eigenvalue of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{v}$
\end_inset

 is the associated eigenvector.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{definition}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In words, an eigenvector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{v}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 does not change its direction when multiplied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 and the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 measures the amount of expansion or shrinkage in this transformation.
 Several remarks regarding Definition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:eigenpair"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 are in order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The eigenpair (i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

the eigenvalue and the eigenvector) is only defined for square matrices.
 This is implicitly assumed in the definition of the eigenpair
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(see
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:eigenpair"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

)).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Every 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

 square matrix has exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 (complex) eigenvalues, possibly with repetitions.
 To see this, one can transform (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:eigenpair"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) into the identity
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\lambda\bm{I}_{n}-\bm{A}\right)\bm{v}=\bm{0}.
\]

\end_inset

Consequently, the scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is an eigenvalue of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathsf{det}\left(\lambda\bm{I}_{n}-\bm{A}\right)=0.
\]

\end_inset

Notice that the above relation specifies a polynomial equation of degree
 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, which by the fundamental theorem of algebra, has exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 roots in the complex field (and hence 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 complex eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A matrix consisting of real entries can have complex eigenvalues and complex
 eigenvectors.
 Take for instance the matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{A}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
3 & -2\\
4 & -1
\end{array}\right],
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $1+2i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1-2i$
\end_inset

 are its two complex eigenvalues.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 denotes the imaginary unit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the last remark being said, there exists one important set of matrices
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

--- 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on
real symmetric matrices
\emph default

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 ---
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of which the eigenvalues must be real.
 This claim is formalized in the following theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Spectral theorem
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:spectral"

\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 be a real symmetric matrix.
 Then there exist 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 real eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1},\cdots\lambda_{n}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and an orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\bm{u}_{1},\bm{u}_{2},\cdots,\bm{u}_{n}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\bm{u}_{i}=\lambda_{i}\bm{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, a real symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 enjoys the following spectral decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{A}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\lambda_{i}\bm{u}_{i}\bm{u}_{i}^{\top},\label{eq:spectral-decomposition-summation-form}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\lambda_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq n}$
\end_inset

 are the real eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\bm{u}_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq n}$
\end_inset

 is an orthonormal basis of
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the eigenvalues of real symmetric matrices are guaranteed to be real
 numbers, we can therefore order them.
 From now on, denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

 the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th largest eigenvalue of a real symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\max}(\bm{A})\triangleq\lambda_{1}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\min}(\bm{A})\triangleq\lambda_{n}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To make the expression
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spectral-decomposition-diagonalization-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) more compact, we denote 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\rvert &  & \rvert\\
\bm{u}_{1} & \cdots & \bm{u}_{n}\\
\rvert &  & \rvert
\end{array}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\bm{\Lambda}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\lambda_{1} & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & \lambda_{2} & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \cdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & 0 & \cdots & \lambda_{n}
\end{array}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}.
\]

\end_inset

As a result, the decomposition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spectral-decomposition-diagonalization-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) can be rewritten as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{A}=\bm{U}\bm{\Lambda}\bm{U}^{\top},\label{eq:spectral-decomposition-multiplication-form}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

in which a real symmetric matrix is decomposed into the multiplications
 of orthonormal matrices (i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

) and a real diagonal matrix (i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

).
 Another useful way to interpret spectral decomposition is through the lens
 of diagonalization.
 Simple algebraic manipulation reveals an equivalent form of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spectral-decomposition-multiplication-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{U}^{\top}\bm{A}\bm{U}=\bm{\Lambda}.\label{eq:spectral-decomposition-diagonalization-form}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This essentially tells us that any real symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 can be 
\emph on
diagonalized 
\emph default
by an orthonormal matrix
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Singular values and singular vectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As we have pointed out in the remarks following Definition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:eigenpair"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, a rectangular matrix does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 have well-defined eigenvalues and eigenvectors.
 It is then natural to ask whether there is a similar decomposition of a
 general matrix to that of a real symmetric matrix (cf.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spectral-decomposition-multiplication-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

)).
 Fortunately, a general matrix admits another useful representation, namely
 the singular value decomposition (SVD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{definition}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Singular value decomposition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "def:SVD"

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 be a general matrix.
 Without loss of generality, assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $m\leq n$
\end_inset

.
 There exists a pair of orthogonal matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{A}=\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top},\label{eq:SVD}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 is a rectangular diagonal matrix with 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{\Sigma}=\left[\begin{array}{cccccc}
\sigma_{1} & 0 & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & 0\\
0 & \sigma_{2} & \cdots & \cdots & 0 & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots\\
0 & 0 & \cdots & \sigma_{m} & 0 & 0
\end{array}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}\geq\sigma_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{m}\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 The decomposition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:SVD"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is called the singular value decomposition, where the nonnegative real
 numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\sigma_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq n}$
\end_inset

 are the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 and the columns in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}$
\end_inset

) are the left (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

right) singular vectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{definition}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the eigenvalues of real symmetric matrices, the singular values
 of a general matrix are guaranteed to be real.
 As a result, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq i\leq\min\{m,n\}$
\end_inset

, we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{i}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th largest singular value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

, and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\max}(\bm{A})\triangleq\sigma_{1}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\min}(\bm{A})\triangleq\sigma_{\min\{m,n\}}(\bm{A})$
\end_inset

) to be the largest (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

smallest) singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is straightforward to read the rank of a matrix from its singular values
 in that the rank is equal to the number of nonzero singular values.
 If the rank 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 of a matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 is smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\min\{m,n\}$
\end_inset

, it is often more economical to represent the matrix using the 
\emph on
compact SVD 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{A}=\bm{U}_{r}\bm{\Sigma}_{r}\bm{V}_{r}^{\top},\label{eq:compact-SVD}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

) is the first 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

 is the upper left block of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 of size 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having introduced two matrix decompositions, it is instrumental to compare
 the singular value decomposition of a general matrix (cf.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

Definition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:SVD"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) with the spectral decomposition of a real symmetric matrix (cf.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

Theorem
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "thm:spectral"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

)).
 First, the eigenvalues of a real symmetric matrix are not necessarily nonnegati
ve whereas singular values must be.
 Second, as claimed in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spectral-decomposition-diagonalization-form"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

), any real symmetric matrix can be diagonalized by a single orthonormal
 matrix.
 However, this may not hold true for a general matrix, nevertheless, SVD
 points out a way to diagonalize a matrix using two orthonormal matrices.
 To see this, one can equate (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:SVD"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}^{\top}\bm{A}\bm{V}=\bm{\Sigma}.
\]

\end_inset

Last but not least, the singular value decomposition and the spectral decomposit
ion are inherently related.
 It is an easy exercise to see that the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}$
\end_inset

) are eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\bm{A}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}$
\end_inset

).
 In addition, the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 are nonnegative square roots of the eigenvalues of either 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\bm{A}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}^{\top}\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 (modulo zero singular values).
 This connection shall prove useful in later discussions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Matrix norms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To measure the size of the perturbation (e.g.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}$
\end_inset

), we need to introduce appropriate metrics
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

/
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

norms on the space of matrices.
 Among all matrix norms, unitarily invariant norms are of most interest
 to us.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{definition}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Unitarily invariant norms
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset

A matrix norm 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 is unitarily invariant if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\bm{A}}=\vertiii{\bm{U}^{\top}\bm{A}\bm{V}}
\]

\end_inset

for any matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
\end_inset

 and any two orthogonal matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{definition}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two prominent examples include the Frobenius norm and the operator norm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}\triangleq\sqrt{\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}^{2}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert \triangleq\max_{\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:\bm{x}\neq\bm{0}}\frac{\left\Vert \bm{A}\bm{x}\right\Vert _{2}}{\left\Vert \bm{x}\right\Vert _{2}}.\label{eq:def-fro-op-norm}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To see why both 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{F}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\cdot\|$
\end_inset

 are unitarily invariant norms, we have the following important characterization
s
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{\min\{m,n\}}\sigma_{i}^{2}\left(\bm{A}\right)}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert =\sigma_{1}\left(\bm{A}\right),
\]

\end_inset

where we recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(\bm{A})\geq\sigma_{2}(\bm{A})\geq\cdots\geq\sigma_{\min\{m,n\}}(\bm{A})\geq0$
\end_inset

 are the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 in descending order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Last but not least, unitarily invariant norms enjoy the following nice propertie
s.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{prop}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

 be any unitarily invariant norm.
 One has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{alignat*}{2}
\vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}} & \leq\vertiii{\bm{A}}\left\Vert \bm{B}\right\Vert _{2}, & \vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}}\leq\vertiii{\bm{B}}\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert _{2}\\
\vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}} & \geq\vertiii{\bm{A}}\sigma_{\min}\left(\bm{B}\right), & \vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}}\geq\vertiii{\bm{B}}\sigma_{\min}\left(\bm{A}\right)
\end{alignat*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}} & \leq\vertiii{\bm{A}}\left\Vert \bm{B}\right\Vert _{2}, &  & \vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}}\leq\vertiii{\bm{B}}\left\Vert \bm{A}\right\Vert _{2}\\
\vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}} & \geq\vertiii{\bm{A}}\sigma_{\min}\left(\bm{B}\right), &  & \vertiii{\bm{A}\bm{B}}\geq\vertiii{\bm{B}}\sigma_{\min}\left(\bm{A}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{prop}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

