\begin_layout Subsection
Perturbation of real symmetric matrices 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Perturbation-of-real-symmetric"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We start with a real symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

, where the focus is its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.
 In light of Theorem
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "thm:spectral"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 has the spectral decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{A}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\lambda_{i}\bm{u}_{i}\bm{u}_{i}^{\top},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset

 are the eigenvalues and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\bm{u}_{i}\}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

--- 
\end_layout

\end_inset

the set of eigenvectors
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 --- 
\end_layout

\end_inset

forms an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 is perturbed by a symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 and we only get to observe 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}=\bm{A}+\bm{E}.$
\end_inset

 Two important questions follow: 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cm{
\end_layout

\end_inset

Need to motivate eigenspaces.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How do the eigenvalues change when the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 is perturbed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
How do the eigenspaces change in response to the perturbation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{E}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before addressing these questions, we introduce some useful notation.
 Denote the spectral decomposition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\bm{A}}=\bm{A}+\bm{E}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\lambda}_{i}\hat{\bm{u}}_{i}\hat{\bm{u}}_{i}^{\top},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{1}\geq\hat{\lambda}_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\hat{\lambda}_{n}$
\end_inset

 are the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{\bm{u}}_{i}\}$
\end_inset

 are the corresponding eigenvectors that are orthonormal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The perturbation of eigenvalues
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With all the notation at hand, we are in position to present the perturbation
 bounds for eigenvalues of symmetric matrices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{lemma}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Weyls's inequality
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "lemma:weyl"

\end_inset

Instate the notation in Section
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Perturbation-of-real-symmetric"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 For every 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq i\leq n$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\lambda_{i}\left(\bm{A}+\bm{E}\right)-\lambda_{i}\left(\bm{A}\right)\right|=\left|\hat{\lambda}_{i}-\lambda_{i}\right|\leq\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert .\label{eq:weyl}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An immediate implication of Lemma
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lemma:weyl"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 is that the eigenvalues of a real symmetric matrix are stable against small
 perturbations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Distance between subspaces
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moving on to the question of the perturbation of eigenvectors
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

/
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

eigenspaces, one clearly needs a distance metric on the space of linear
 subspaces.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

 be two linear subspaces of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 with dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

) be an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

).
 This subsection is centered around quantifying the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

.
 One naive idea is to use the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

metric
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\bm{U}_{0}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

 is any norm on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$
\end_inset

.
 However, an obvious drawback of this approach is that it does not take
 into account the rotation ambiguity that is inherent in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 --- 
\end_layout

\end_inset

for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{R}\in\mathcal{O}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

, the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\bm{R}$
\end_inset

 is also an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, any reasonable distance metric should handle the rotation ambiguity
 properly.
 Below are two popular metrics to measure the distance between a pair of
 subspaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
The algebraic construction of distance metrics
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 --- 
\end_layout

\end_inset

projection matrices.
 
\emph default
It is an established fact in linear algebra that the projection matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

) onto the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

) is unique.
 Utilizing this fact, one can define the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{\vertiii{\cdot}}\left(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}\right)\triangleq\vertiii{\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}},\label{eq:algebraic-dist}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

 is some norm on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

.
 It is an exercise for the readers to prove that this is indeed a valid
 distance metric on the space of linear subspaces.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
The geometric construction of distance metrics
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 --- 
\end_layout

\end_inset

canonical angles
\emph default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{Y}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 be the SVD of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X},\bm{Y}\in\mathcal{O}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

 are orthonormal matrices and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}=\mathsf{diag}(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\cdots,\sigma_{r})$
\end_inset

 consisting of the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

 in a descending order.
 It is easy to check that all the singular values 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\sigma_{i}\}$
\end_inset

 are contained in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, one can define the 
\emph on
canonical angles
\emph default
 between two subspaces spanned by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

) to be 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{i}\triangleq\cos^{-1}\left(\sigma_{i}\right)\in\left[0,\pi/2\right],\qquad\text{for all}\quad1\leq i\leq r.
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq\theta_{1}\leq\theta_{2}\leq\cdots\leq\theta_{r}\leq\pi/2$
\end_inset

.
 To see why this definition makes sense, let us consider the simplest case
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $r=1$
\end_inset

 and hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}$
\end_inset

 are two unit vectors.
 In this case, the canonical angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is exactly the angle between the two unit vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}$
\end_inset

: when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}=0$
\end_inset

, the two subspaces coincide with each other and when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}=\pi/2$
\end_inset

, they are far way from each other.
 Based on this observation, the set of canonical angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\theta_{i}\}$
\end_inset

 forms a good basis to measure the distance between two subspaces.
 To formalize this idea, we arrange the canonical angles into a diagonal
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{\Theta}\triangleq\mathsf{diag}\left(\theta_{1},\theta_{2},\cdots,\theta_{r}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r},
\]

\end_inset

and correspondingly define 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\sin\bm{\Theta} & \triangleq\mathsf{diag}\left(\sin\theta_{1},\sin\theta_{2},\cdots,\sin\theta_{r}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

With this notation in place, one can measure the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

 through 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cm{
\end_layout

\end_inset

May not be a good notation to use due to the ignorance of the norm.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{\sin}\left(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}\right)=\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}},\label{eq:geometric-dist}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

 is some norm on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
As we will soon see, the algebraic distance (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:algebraic-dist"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and the geometric distance (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:geometric-dist"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) are closely related.
 This connection is best illustrated via the following lemma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "lemma:connection-algebraic-geometric"

\end_inset

Denote
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k=\begin{cases}
r, & \text{if }2r\leq n,\\
n-r, & \text{if }2r>n.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Then the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\underbrace{\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r-1},\sin\theta_{r-1},\cdots,\sin\theta_{r+1-k},\sin\theta_{r+1-k}}_{2k},\;\underbrace{0,0,\cdots,0}_{n-2k}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $2r\leq n$
\end_inset

.
 Then the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\underbrace{\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r-1},\sin\theta_{r-1},\cdots,\sin\theta_{1},\sin\theta_{1}}_{2r},\;\underbrace{0,0,\cdots,0}_{n-2r}.
\]

\end_inset

Similarly, when 
\begin_inset Formula $2r>n$
\end_inset

, the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\underbrace{\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r-1},\sin\theta_{r-1},\cdots,\sin\theta_{2r+1-n},\sin\theta_{2r+1-n}}_{2(n-r)},\;\underbrace{0,0,\cdots,0}_{2r-n}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(n-r)}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(n-r)}$
\end_inset

) be an orthonormal basis for the orthogonal complement to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

 (reps.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

).
 Since singular values are unitarily invariant, we turn to investigating
 the singlur values of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\\
\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}
\end{array}\right]\left(\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\right)\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{\bm{U}}_{1} & \hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1} & \bm{0}\\
\bm{0} & -\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

As result, the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 are composed of those of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Recall from our discussion on the relationship between eigenvalues and
 singular values.
 We turn to studying the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\left(\bm{I}_{n}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\right)\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{X}\left(\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{\Sigma}^{2}\right)\bm{X}^{\top},
\]

\end_inset

where the last relation uses the SVD 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{Y}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 It is then easy to check that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{i}(\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1})=\sqrt{\lambda_{i}(\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0})}=\sqrt{1-\cos^{2}\theta_{r+1-i}}=\sin\theta_{r+1-i}.
\]

\end_inset

Similaly, the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $-\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

 are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\sin\theta_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq r}$
\end_inset

.
 Combine these two observations to complete the proof.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lemma
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lemma:connection-algebraic-geometric"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 connects the singular values of the difference of projection matrices with
 the sines of canonical angles between subspaces.
 With the help of Lemma
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lemma:connection-algebraic-geometric"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, one can easily obtain the following corollary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{corollary}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "prop:unitary-norm-property"

\end_inset

One has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\Vert \bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\right\Vert  & =\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\right\Vert \qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\Vert \bm{U}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}=\sqrt{2}\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{corollary}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Perturbation of eigenspaces: Davis-Kahan's sin-
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now proceed to the perturbation bounds for eigenspaces.
 Unlike eigenvalues, which are stable against small perturbations, the eigenspac
e of a real symmetric matrix can change drastically even under a small perturbat
ion.
 For instance, take
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{A}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1+\epsilon & 0\\
0 & 1-\epsilon
\end{array}\right],\quad\bm{E}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\epsilon & \epsilon\\
\epsilon & \epsilon
\end{array}\right],\quad\text{and}\quad\hat{\bm{A}}=\bm{A}+\bm{E}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & \epsilon\\
\epsilon & 1
\end{array}\right],
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 is a small positive constant.
 It is straightforward to compute that the leading eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 (reps.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}$
\end_inset

) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}_{1}=(1,0)^{\top}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{u}}_{1}=(1/\sqrt{2},1/\sqrt{2})^{\top}$
\end_inset

).
 As a result, the sin
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Theta}$
\end_inset

 distance between the subspaces spanned by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{u}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{u}}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

, which can be arbitrarily large compared with the small perturbation 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 On closer inspection, this pathological behavior exists due to the fact
 that size 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 of the perturbation is comparable to the eigengap of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

, i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}(\bm{A})-\lambda_{2}(\bm{A})=2\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 This motivates us to impose conditions on the eigengap such that the eigenspace
 could be stable against perturbations.
 The stability of eigenspaces is formally stated in Theorem
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "thm:davis-kahan"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Davis-Kahan sin-
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 theorem
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:davis-kahan"

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{A}}=\bm{A}+\bm{E}$
\end_inset

 be two symmetric matrices in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 with the spectral decomposition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bm{A} & =\bm{U}\bm{\Lambda}\bm{U}^{\top}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\bm{U}_{0} & \bm{U}_{1}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\bm{\Lambda}_{0} & \bm{0}\\
\bm{0} & \bm{\Lambda}_{1}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\\
\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}
\end{array}\right];\\
\hat{\bm{A}} & =\hat{\bm{U}}\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}\hat{\bm{U}}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{\bm{U}}_{0} & \hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{0} & \bm{0}\\
\bm{0} & \hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\\
\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}
\end{array}\right],
\end{align*}

\end_inset

respectively.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\bm{\Lambda}_{0} & =\mathsf{diag}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\cdots\lambda_{r}\right),\qquad\bm{\Lambda}_{1}=\mathsf{diag}\left(\lambda_{r+1},\lambda_{r+2},\cdots\lambda_{n}\right),\\
\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{0} & =\mathsf{diag}\big(\hat{\lambda}_{1},\hat{\lambda}_{2},\cdots\hat{\lambda}_{r}\big),\qquad\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}=\mathsf{diag}\big(\hat{\lambda}_{r+1},\hat{\lambda}_{r+2},\cdots\hat{\lambda}_{n}\big),
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{r}>\lambda_{r+1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{1}\geq\hat{\lambda}_{2}\geq\cdots\geq\hat{\lambda}_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the perturbation is not too large in the sense that 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{E}\|<\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then we have for any unitarily invariant norm 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}\big(\bm{U}_{0},\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\big)}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}_{0}}}{\lambda_{r}-\left(\lambda_{r+1}+\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert \right)}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Consider the settings in Section 2.2.1.
 Assume that all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 lie in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[\alpha,\beta]$
\end_inset

 and those of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}$
\end_inset

 lie outside of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha-\Delta,\beta+\Delta)$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}^{\star}}}{\Delta}.
\]

\end_inset

The same conclusion holds true if the roles of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}$
\end_inset

 are switched.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Without loss of generality, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=-\beta$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Two immediate consequences are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}\|\leq\beta,\qquad\text{and}\qquad\|\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}^{-1}\|\leq(\beta+\delta)^{-1}.
\]

\end_inset

Define the residual matrix 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{R}\coloneqq\left(\bm{M}-\bm{M}^{\star}\right)\bm{U}^{\star}=\bm{M}\bm{U}^{\star}-\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}.
\]

\end_inset

Multiply both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{R}=\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}-\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}.
\]

\end_inset

Invoke the triangle inequality to see that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{R}} & \geq\vertiii{\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}-\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}}\\
 & \geq\frac{1}{\|\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}^{-1}\|}\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}-\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}\|\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}\|\\
 & \geq(\beta+\delta)\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}-\beta\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}=\delta\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Here the middle line uses the facts that 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}=\vertiii{\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}^{-1}\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}\leq\|\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}^{-1}\|\vertiii{\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}$
\end_inset

 and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}}\leq\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}\|\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}\|$
\end_inset

; see Prop XXX.
 The last inequality arises from the estimates (XXX).
 As a consequence, we arrive at the conclusion that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{R}}}{\delta}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{R}}}{\delta}=\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}^{\star}}}{\delta}.
\]

\end_inset

Identifying 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}}$
\end_inset

 concludes the proof.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{corollary}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Consider the setting where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 contains all nonzero eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
 Accordingly, assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

 contains the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 largest eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

 in the absolute value sense.
 Then if 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{E}\|\leq1/2\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|$
\end_inset

, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}\big(\bm{U}^{\star},\bm{U}\big)}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}^{\star}}}{\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|-\|\bm{E}\|}.
\]

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{corollary}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

In view of Weyl's inequality, we know that for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2,\cdots,n$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|\lambda_{i}(\bm{M})-\lambda_{i}(\bm{M}^{\star})|\leq\|\bm{E}\|\leq\frac{1}{2}\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|,
\]

\end_inset

which immediately implies that all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}_{\perp}$
\end_inset

 lie within the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-\|\bm{E}\|,\|\bm{E}\|]$
\end_inset

.
 This together with the assumption that all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 are in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}\backslash(-\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|,\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|)$
\end_inset

 allows us to invoke Theorem XXX to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}^{\star}}}{\min_{1\leq i\leq k}|\lambda_{i}^{\star}|-\|\bm{E}\|}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

In view of the decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}=\bm{U}_{0}\bm{\Lambda}_{0}\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}+\bm{U}_{1}\bm{\Lambda}_{1}\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 and the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{0}$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{A}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{U}_{0}\bm{\Lambda}_{0},
\]

\end_inset

which further implies 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{A}\bm{U}_{0}=\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\bm{\Lambda}_{0}.\label{eq:dk-identity-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In addition, the similar decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}+\bm{E}=\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}+\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 and the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}=\bm{0}$
\end_inset

 tell us that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bm{A}+\bm{E})\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}=\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and hence 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\left(\bm{A}+\bm{E}\right)\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}=\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}.\label{eq:dk-identity-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Combine the two identities (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dk-identity-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dk-identity-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) to reach 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{E}\bm{U}_{0} & =\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\bm{\Lambda}_{0}\nonumber \\
 & =\big(\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}-c\bm{I}_{n-r}\big)\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\left(\bm{\Lambda}_{0}-c\bm{I}_{r}\right),\label{eq:dk-identity-main}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where the last line holds for any 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, we can take 
\begin_inset Formula $c\triangleq(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{r})/2$
\end_inset

 and denote 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\triangleq(\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{r})/2\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Lambda}_{0}-c\bm{I}_{r}$
\end_inset

 are contained in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $[-\rho,\rho]$
\end_inset

 whereas all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}-c\bm{I}_{n-r}$
\end_inset

 are smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{r+1}-c$
\end_inset

.
 Apply Weyl's inequality (see Lemma
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lemma:weyl"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) to see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{r+1}\leq\lambda_{r+1}+\|\bm{E}\|$
\end_inset

.
 This combined with relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{r+1}-c=\lambda_{r+1}-\lambda_{r}-\rho$
\end_inset

 reveals that all the eigenvalues of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}-c\bm{I}_{n-r}$
\end_inset

 are within 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\infty,\lambda_{r+1}-\lambda_{r}+\|\bm{E}\|-\rho)$
\end_inset

.
 Two immediate consequences are that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \bm{\Lambda}_{0}-c\bm{I}_{r}\right\Vert \le\rho,\qquad\text{and}\qquad\sigma_{\min}\big(\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}-c\bm{I}_{n-r}\big)\geq\rho+\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}-\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert .\label{eq:dk-relation-main}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Continue the derivation in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dk-identity-main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{E}\bm{U}_{0}} & \overset{(\text{i})}{\geq}\vertiii{\left(\hat{\bm{\Lambda}}_{1}-c\bm{I}_{n-r}\right)\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}}-\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\left(\bm{\Lambda}_{0}-c\bm{I}_{r}\right)}\\
 & \overset{(\text{ii})}{\geq}\left(\rho+\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}-\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert _{\mathsf{}}\right)\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}}-\rho\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}}\\
 & =\left(\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}-\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert _{\mathsf{}}\right)\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}},
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where (i) follows from the triangle inequality and (ii) holds because of
 Proposition
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "prop:unitary-norm-property"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 and the conditions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:dk-relation-main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Clearly, the above inequality is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}}\leq\frac{\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{E}\bm{U}_{0}}}{\lambda_{r}-\lambda_{r+1}-\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert }\leq\frac{\vertiii{\bm{E}\bm{U}_{0}}}{\lambda_{r}-\left(\lambda_{r+1}+\left\Vert \bm{E}\right\Vert \right)}.
\]

\end_inset

The proof is then completed via observing Lemma
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lemma:sin-theta-equivalence"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "lemma:sin-theta-equivalence"

\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $[\bm{U}_{0},\bm{U}_{1}]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $[\hat{\bm{U}}_{0},\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}]$
\end_inset

 are orthonormal matrices.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\theta_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq r}$
\end_inset

 be the canonical angles between the space spanned by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

 and that by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Then the singular values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}$
\end_inset

 are given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sin\theta_{r},\sin\theta_{r-1},\cdots\sin\theta_{1},\;0,0,\cdots0.
\]

\end_inset

Consequently, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\left(\bm{U}_{0},\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\right)\right\Vert =\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\right\Vert \qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\left(\bm{U}_{0},\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\right)\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}=\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}.\label{eq:sin-theta-equivalence}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Moreover, for any unitarily invariant norm 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\cdot}$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}\left(\bm{U}_{0},\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\right)}=\vertiii{\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Consider the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\big(\bm{I}_{n}-\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\big)\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}.
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{Y}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 be the SVD of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 By definition, one has 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}=\mathsf{diag}(\cos\theta_{1},\cos\theta_{2},\cdots,\cos\theta_{r})$
\end_inset

.
 Continue the derivation above to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{U}_{0}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}=\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}^{2}\bm{X}^{\top}=\bm{X}\left(\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{\Sigma}^{2}\right)\bm{X}^{\top}.
\]

\end_inset

Utilizing the relationship between singular values and eigenvalues, one
 sees that for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq i\leq r$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{i}\big(\hat{\bm{U}}_{1}^{\top}\bm{U}_{0}\big)=\sqrt{\lambda_{i}\left(\bm{I}_{r}-\bm{\Sigma}^{2}\right)}=\sqrt{\lambda_{i}\left(1-\cos^{2}\theta_{r-i+1}\right)}=\sin\theta_{r-i+1}.
\]

\end_inset

This finishes the first part.
 The consequences in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sin-theta-equivalence"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) follow immediately.
 The last relation needs more care.
