\begin_layout Section
Notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter, we have reviewed classical matrix perturbation theory that
 are useful for our subsequent developments on spectral methods.
 We conclude this chapter with additional references on this subject.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our exposition largely follows the excellent book by Stewart 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "stewart1990matrix"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Two other classical references are 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bhatia2013matrix,horn2012matrix"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, which go beyond matrix perturbation theory and deal with general matrix
 analysis.
 More broadly, matrices can be viewed as linear operators on the finite
 dimensional Euclidean space; the general treatment of perturbation theory
 for linear operators can be found in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kato2013perturbation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that in Lemma 2.2 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cm{
\end_layout

\end_inset

Need to change later
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 we have stated the equivalence between 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}\|$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}\|_{\mathrm{F}}$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\sin\bm{\Theta}\|$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\|\sin\bm{\Theta}\|_{\mathrm{F}}$
\end_inset

).
 It turns out that 
\begin_inset Formula $\vertiii{\bm{U}_{\perp}^{\top}\bm{U}^{\star}}=\vertiii{\sin\bm{\Theta}}$
\end_inset

 holds for any unitarily invariant norm; see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Lemma 2.2"
key "li1998relative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "Theorems 3.2 and 3.4"
key "li1998relative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 offer general versions of Davis-Kahan's and Wedin's theorems, which do
 not restrict attention to the top eigenspaces or singular subspaces.
 A user-friendly version of the Davis-Kahan theorem was provided in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "MR3371006"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the Procrustes problem 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathsf{minimize}_{\bm{R}\in\mathcal{O}^{r\times r}}\quad\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\bm{R}-\bm{U}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2},
\]

\end_inset

whose solution is revealed in the SVD of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\bm{U}$
\end_inset

.
 More specifically, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{Y}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 be the SVD of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\bm{U}$
\end_inset

, then one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{X}\bm{Y}^{\top}=\arg\min_{\bm{R}\in\mathcal{O}^{r\times r}}\quad\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\bm{R}-\bm{U}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}.
\]

\end_inset

Denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\cdots\sigma_{r})$
\end_inset

 the entries in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 in decreasing order.
 It is easy to check that they are contained in 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, we define the principal angles between two subspaces spaned
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\bm{U}}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{i}\triangleq\cos^{-1}\left(\sigma_{i}\right),\qquad\text{for all}\qquad1\leq i\leq r
\]

\end_inset

and arrange them into a diagonal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{\Theta}\triangleq\mathsf{diag}\left(\theta_{1},\theta_{2},\cdots,\theta_{r}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}.
\]

\end_inset

With 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{\Theta}$
\end_inset

 in place, we can define 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\cos\bm{\Theta} & =\mathsf{diag}\left(\cos\theta_{1},\cos\theta_{2},\cdots,\cos\theta_{r}\right)=\bm{\Sigma};\\
\sin\bm{\Theta} & =\mathsf{diag}\left(\sin\theta_{1},\sin\theta_{2},\cdots,\sin\theta_{r}\right).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several immediate facts are in order.
 First 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\left(\sin\theta_{r}\right)^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\left[1-\left(\cos\theta_{r}\right)^{2}\right]=r-\left\Vert \bm{\Sigma}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}.
\]

\end_inset

Second, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\bm{X}\bm{Y}^{\top}-\bm{U}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2} & =\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}+\left\Vert \bm{U}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}-2\mathsf{Tr}\left(\bm{Y}\bm{X}^{\top}\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\bm{U}\right)\\
 & =2r-2\mathsf{Tr}\left(\bm{Y}\bm{X}^{\top}\bm{X}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{Y}^{\top}\right)\\
 & =2r-2\mathsf{Tr}\left(\bm{\Sigma}\right)\\
 & \leq2r-2\sum_{i=1}^{r}\sigma_{i}^{2}\\
 & =2\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Last but not least, the distance between projection matrices satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}-\bm{U}\bm{U}^{\top}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2} & =\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}+\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}-2\mathsf{Tr}\left(\hat{\bm{U}}\hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\bm{U}\bm{U}^{\top}\right)\\
 & =2r-2\left\Vert \hat{\bm{U}}^{\top}\bm{U}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\\
 & =2r-2\left\Vert \bm{\Sigma}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\\
 & =2\left\Vert \sin\bm{\Theta}\right\Vert _{\mathrm{F}}^{2}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Matrix completion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imagine that one observes a small subset of the entries of a matrix and
 seeks to recover the full matrix.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
cm{
\end_layout

\end_inset

Add a few words on applications.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times n_{2}}$
\end_inset

 be the rank-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 matrix we hope to recover.
 Without loss of generality, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}\leq n_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\subseteq\{1,2\cdots n_{1}\}]\times\{1,2,\cdots,n_{2}\}$
\end_inset

 the index set for the observed entries, and correpondingly define the projectio
n operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}:\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times n_{2}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times n_{2}}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{A})]_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
A_{i,j}, & \text{if }(i,j)\in\Omega,\\
0, & \text{else},
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times n_{2}}$
\end_inset

 denotes the input matrix.
 With this notation in place, matrix completion amounts to recovering 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 from its partial entries 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})$
\end_inset

.
 Throughout this paper, we impose the Bernoulli observation model detailed
 in the following assumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{assumption}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Bernoulli observation model for matrix completion
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset

We assume that each entry in 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 is observed independentlywith probability 
\begin_inset Formula $0<p\leq1$
\end_inset

, i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)\in\Omega$
\end_inset

 independently with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq i\leq n_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leq j\leq n_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{assumption}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The random observation model alone does not guarantee effective recovery
 of the underlying matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
 It turns out the following incoherence property of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 plays a crutial role in recovering low-rank matrices from partial observations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{definition}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Incoherence for matrix completion
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset

A rank-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times n_{2}}$
\end_inset

 with compact SVD 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}=\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\bm{V}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

-incoherent for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\geq1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|\bm{U}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{n_{1}}}\|\bm{U}^{\star}\|_{\mathrm{F}}=\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}}},\quad\text{and}\quad\|\bm{V}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{n_{2}}}\|\bm{V}^{\star}\|_{\mathrm{F}}=\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{2}}}.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{definition}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before introducing the spectral method for matrix completion, we single
 out a few immediate consequences of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

-incoherence.
 First, the maximum row norm 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 cannot be two large, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}=\|\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\bm{V}^{\star\top}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\|\bm{U}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\|\|\bm{V}^{\star}\|\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}),\label{eq:mc-row-norm-upper}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first inequality arises from the elementary inequalities 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{A}\bm{B}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\|\bm{A}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{B}\|$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{A}\bm{B}\|\leq\|\bm{A}\|\|\bm{B}\|$
\end_inset

.
 The last relation uses the incoherence assumption on 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

, the orthogonality of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}^{\star}$
\end_inset

, and identifies 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\|$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star})$
\end_inset

.
 An analogous upper bound holds for 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{M}^{\star\top}\|_{2,\infty}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{M}^{\star\top}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{V}^{\star}\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\bm{U}^{\star\top}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\|\bm{V}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\|\|\bm{U}^{\star}\|\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}).\label{eq:mc-column-norm-upper}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In addition, the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 is element-wise bounded in the sense that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{\infty}=\|\bm{U}^{\star}\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\bm{V}^{\star\top}\|_{\infty}\leq\|\bm{U}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{\Sigma}^{\star}\|\|\bm{V}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\frac{\mu r}{\sqrt{n_{1}n_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}).\label{eq:mc-entry-upper}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, the first inequality follows from the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{A}\bm{B}^{\top}\|_{\infty}\leq\|\bm{A}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{B}\|_{2,\infty}$
\end_inset

 and the aforementioned 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{A}\bm{B}\|_{2,\infty}\leq\|\bm{A}\|_{2,\infty}\|\bm{B}\|$
\end_inset

, while the last one again utilizes the incoherence assumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Spectral method for matrix completion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that the idea of spectral method is to first form a good approximation
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

 to the unknown matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 we care about, and then perform spectral decomposition on 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}$
\end_inset

 to approximately recover the singular spaces (spectral properties) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
 Given our random sampling model, such a good approximation is readily available
: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bm{M}\coloneqq p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star}).
\]

\end_inset

Indeed, one can check that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}[\bm{M}]=\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 where the expectation is taken over the randomness in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 As a result, it is reasonble to use 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}$
\end_inset

, the top-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 SVD of 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})$
\end_inset

 as an approximation to the groundtruth 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
 We learned from Wedin's 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin\Theta$
\end_inset

 theorem that whether or not 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}$
\end_inset

) is close to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 (resp.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\bm{V}^{\star}$
\end_inset

) relies crutially on the size of the perturbation 
\begin_inset Formula $\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore we begin with offering an upper bound on 
\begin_inset Formula $\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{2}p\geq C\mu r\log n_{2}$
\end_inset

 for some sufficiently large constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n_{2}^{-10})$
\end_inset

, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}\,\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}).
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{lemma}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Note that the term 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 can be expressed as the sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}n_{2}$
\end_inset

 i.i.d.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

random matrices:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\underbrace{(p^{-1}\mathbbm{1}\{\delta_{ij}=1\}-1)M_{ij}^{\star}\bm{e}_{i}\bm{e}_{j}^{\top}}_{\eqqcolon\bm{X}_{ij}},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{ij}$
\end_inset

 follows an independent Bernoulli distribution with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{e}_{i}$
\end_inset

 stands for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th standard basis vector.
 It is easily seen that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i,j$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}[\bm{X}_{ij}]=\bm{0},\quad\text{and}\quad\|\bm{X}_{ij}\|\leq p^{-1}\|\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{\infty}\leq\frac{\mu r}{p\sqrt{n_{1}n_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}),
\]

\end_inset

where the last relation uses the entrywise upper bound (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mc-entry-upper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) on 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

.
 In order to apply the matrix Bernstein inequality, we need to control the
 variance statistics
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v\coloneqq\max\left\{ \left\Vert \sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{X}_{ij}\bm{X}_{ij}^{\top}\right]\right\Vert ,\left\Vert \sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{X}_{ij}^{\top}\bm{X}_{ij}\right]\right\Vert \right\} .
\]

\end_inset

Regarding the first variance term, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{X}_{ij}\bm{X}_{ij}^{\top}\right] & =\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\mathbb{E}\left[(p^{-1}\mathbbm{1}\{\delta_{ij}=1\}-1)^{2}(M_{ij}^{\star})^{2}\bm{e}_{i}\bm{e}_{j}^{\top}\bm{e}_{j}\bm{e}_{i}^{\top}\right]\\
 & =\frac{1-p}{p}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}(M_{ij}^{\star})^{2}\bm{e}_{i}\bm{e}_{i}^{\top}=\frac{1-p}{p}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\|\bm{M}_{i,\cdot}^{\star}\|_{2}^{2}\bm{e}_{i}\bm{e}_{i}^{\top}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Here, the first identity arises from the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{X}_{ij}$
\end_inset

, the second one calculated the variance of a Bernoulli random variable,
 and the last equation follows from a direct calculation.
 Similarly, the second term in the variance statistics enjoys the following
 characterization:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{X}_{ij}^{\top}\bm{X}_{ij}\right]=\frac{1-p}{p}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\|\bm{M}_{\cdot,j}^{\star}\|_{2}^{2}\bm{e}_{j}\bm{e}_{j}^{\top}.
\]

\end_inset

Taking the above two relations together, we obtain the following upper bound
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
v & \leq\frac{1}{p}\max\left\{ \|\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{2,\infty}^{2},\|\bm{M}^{\star\top}\|_{2,\infty}^{2}\right\} \leq\frac{1}{p}\left\{ \frac{\mu r}{n_{1}}\sigma_{1}^{2}(\bm{M}^{\star}),\frac{\mu r}{n_{2}}\sigma_{1}^{2}(\bm{M}^{\star})\right\} \leq\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\sigma_{1}^{2}(\bm{M}^{\star}),
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where the middle relation stems from the upper bounds (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mc-row-norm-upper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mc-column-norm-upper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) and the last inequality uses the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}\leq n_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Now we are in position to invoke the matrix Bernstein inequality and arrive
 at the conclusion that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\sigma_{1}^{2}(\bm{M}^{\star})\log n_{2}}+\frac{\mu r}{p\sqrt{n_{1}n_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star})\log n_{2}
\]

\end_inset

holds with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n^{-10})$
\end_inset

.
 To further simplify the matter, note that when 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{2}p\geq C\mu r\log n_{2}$
\end_inset

 for some sufficiently large constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C>0$
\end_inset

, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\mu r}{p\sqrt{n_{1}n_{2}}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star})\log n_{2}\leq\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\sigma_{1}^{2}(\bm{M}^{\star})\log n_{2}},
\]

\end_inset

which further implies the desired claim.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a consequence of Lemma XXX, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star})\le\frac{1}{2}\sigma_{r}(\bm{M}^{\star}),
\]

\end_inset

as long as 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}p\geq C_{1}\kappa^{2}\mu r\log n_{2}$
\end_inset

 for some sufficiently large constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{1}>0$
\end_inset

.
 We are now prepared to apply Wedin's 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin\Theta$
\end_inset

 theorem to obtain the following upper bounds on the singular space variations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Spectral method for matrix completion
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{1}p\geq C_{1}\kappa^{2}\mu r\log n_{2}$
\end_inset

 for some sufficiently large constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{1}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then with probability exceeding 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n^{-10})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\max\left\{ \mathsf{dist}\left(\bm{U},\bm{U}^{\star}\right),\mathsf{dist}\left(\bm{V},\bm{V}^{\star}\right)\right\}  & \lesssim\kappa\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}};\\
\max\left\{ \mathsf{dist}_{\mathrm{F}}\left(\bm{U},\bm{U}^{\star}\right),\mathsf{dist}_{\mathrm{F}}\left(\bm{V},\bm{V}^{\star}\right)\right\}  & \lesssim\kappa\sqrt{\frac{\mu r^{2}}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}[
\end_layout

\end_inset

Matrix completion: recovered matrix
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

]
\end_layout

\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{2}p\geq C\mu r\log n_{2}$
\end_inset

 for some sufficiently large constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then with probability at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-O(n_{2}^{-10})$
\end_inset

, one has 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}\| & \lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}\,\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}),\quad\text{and}\quad\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{\mathrm{F}}\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r^{2}}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}\,\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}$
\end_inset

 is the best rank-
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 approximation to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})$
\end_inset

, i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})\|=\min_{\bm{A}:\mathsf{rank}(\bm{A})\leq r}\|\bm{A}-p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})\|$
\end_inset

.
 As a consequence, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\leq\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})\|+\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\leq2\|p^{-1}\mathcal{P}_{\Omega}(\bm{M}^{\star})-\bm{M}^{\star}\|,
\]

\end_inset

where the first relation is the triangle inequality.
 This combined with Lemma XXX finishes the proof of the first part.
 Regarding the second part, i.e.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

~
\end_layout

\end_inset

the bound on the Frobenius norm, we make the observation that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}$
\end_inset

 has rank at most 
\begin_inset Formula $2r$
\end_inset

, which immediately implies 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}\|_{\mathrm{F}}\leq\sqrt{2r}\|\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}\bm{V}^{\top}-\bm{M}^{\star}\|\lesssim\sqrt{\frac{\mu r^{2}}{n_{1}p}\log n_{2}}\sigma_{1}(\bm{M}^{\star}).
\]

\end_inset

This completes the proof of the second part.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

